\description{
Plots the model's probability of each response category against the
latent variable \eqn{\theta}, one panel per item. The item parameters
are estimated by conditional maximum likelihood with
\code{psychotools::pcmodel()} (a dichotomous item is a 2-category partial
credit model). The curves are coloured from low to high categories with
a viridis palette. Comparable to \code{eRm::plotICC()} and the trace plots of
\pkg{mirt}, with optional labels for items and categories.
}
\details{
For each polytomous item \emph{i} with response categories
\eqn{0, 1, \ldots, K_i} and threshold parameters
\eqn{\delta_{i,1}, \ldots, \delta_{i,K_i}} (CML, \code{psychotools}),
the PCM category probability is

\deqn{P(X_i = k \mid \theta) = \frac{\exp(\sum_{j=1}^{k}
  (\theta - \delta_{i,j}))}{\sum_{k'=0}^{K_i} \exp(\sum_{j=1}^{k'}
  (\theta - \delta_{i,j}))},}

with the empty sum (when \eqn{k = 0}) taken as zero. A dichotomous
item has a single threshold, its difficulty, which gives the usual
logistic item characteristic curve.

The colours follow the category number, so low categories are at one
end of the palette and high categories at the other. With
\code{category_labels}, the legend shows those labels (for example "Never",
"Sometimes", "Often").

An item with fewer categories than the others (for example one
three-category item in a four-category scale) shows only its own
categories. The y-axis still spans \eqn{[0, 1]}.
}
